# The filesystem kernel/test/mount mounts on /mnt -- task C4f.
#
# IT IS NOT THE ROOT, for kernel/test/fsck.manifest's reason and one more of this task's
# own: this volume is MOUNTED and then written through the buffer cache, and a mount can
# only ever be a second filesystem here -- NMOUNT is 2 and iinit() has taken mount[0].
#
# THE I-NUMBERS ARE THIS FILE'S ORDER, which mount.sh depends on twice: it clri's inode 7
# and expects fsck to find the entry that still names it.  b6fsutil allocates in the order
# the stanzas appear, so
#
#	2 /          3 /lost+found	4 /d	5 /d/e	 6 /motd     7 /d/e/f
#
# and ANY STANZA ADDED ABOVE ONE OF THOSE RENUMBERS EVERYTHING BELOW IT.  kernel/test/
# simh.damage carries the same warning for the same reason, and run-mount.sh's oracle 1
# would catch the drift only if the resulting filesystem were also broken, which it would
# not be -- so read this before editing.
#
#   /lost+found   fsck's reconnect target.  Empty is enough; ../../root.manifest's stanza
#                 for the same directory says why.
#   /d/e/f        the file mount.sh clears, three levels down so that ncheck has a path
#                 worth printing and fsck's phase 2 has something to descend.
#   /motd         one more file, so that the i-list and the free list are not trivial, and
#                 the thing the guest reads back through the mount.
#
# The `source' paths resolve against b6fsutil's working directory, build/kernel/test, as
# ../../root.manifest's do.  /etc/motd is used because it is already staged there.

default
owner 0
group 0
dirmode 0755
filemode 0644

dir /lost+found
dir /d
dir /d/e

file /motd
source ../../rootfs/etc/motd

file /d/e/f
owner 7
source ../../rootfs/etc/motd
